WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.001
-Is this a positive for you?

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.903
I mean, you grew up
in South Central.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:06.572
You've seen this your whole
life, as you've mentioned.

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:09.776
Now there are people from all
different walks of life

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.912
who are addressing this issue,

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:16.048
at least saying they care about
this issue and want solutions.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:17.450
Is that important to you?

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.718
Have you seen this before,

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.887
and are you hopeful that it will
be different this time?

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:24.023
-I mean, let's be honest.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:26.592
I am so happy finally

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:29.995
that people are seeing
both sides of the coin

00:00:29.995 --> 00:00:33.065
and that they're out there
marching and protesting.

00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:36.369
And that's great.
That's great.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:38.705
But there needs to be
more effective change made.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.173
There needs to be more done.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:41.807
And I'm grateful and thankful

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:44.310
that people see what we are
going through over here

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.378
and they have understanding

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:46.880
and they're like
"This has to stop."

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.547
I'm so grateful.

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:50.016
I'm grateful and thankful.

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:51.818
But you all need to be making
these phone calls

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:56.756
to these politicians,
to these board supervisors

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.392
and the mayor
and the councilmen.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.260
There needs to be change made,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.430
and there needs to be
an effective way

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.632
of dismantling the system
of oppression,

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:08.801
the syst-- systemic --

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.003
You know what I'm saying!

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:12.205
The racism has been
going on for a long time!

00:01:12.205 --> 00:01:14.373
-[ Laughs ]
-It needs to be stopped.

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:17.377
-Hey, you and I are obviously --
we're in show business.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:20.980
I have seen so many cop movies,
cop shows over the years.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.883
I would call myself a fan
of those kind of shows.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.186
And yet I realize
as we go through this,

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:28.621
so many of the cops
that are framed as heroes

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:32.591
often make choices that
require them crossing a line.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:34.593
Because we see it from their
perspective, we think,

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:37.830
"Well, they know the right time
to cross the line."

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.333
Do you worry that the way
police are perceived

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:42.702
and growing up like you did
in South Central...

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:44.103
[ Dog barking ]
...was that something

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:45.405
that was always strange to you?
-I'm sorry, hold up.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.640
I'm sorry.
There is a man at my door.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:51.310
And my guard dog, the Maltese,
is losing it.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.380
That's my little white dog.
She protects me. Hold up.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.816
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:02.255
[ Door opens ]

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.456
-Thank you.

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:07.126
[ Door closes ]

00:02:07.126 --> 00:02:08.527
Sorry, Seth.

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:10.363
-That's alright, Tiffany.

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.798
-I'm sorry, but the dog
would've barked the whole time

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.700
until I answered the door.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.203
-How -- Now,
I'm not a dog expert,

00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:21.140
but based on the bark,
that's not a large dog.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.043
-No. She's like 7 pounds.
Sleeper!

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:26.679
This is my baby girl right here.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.781
Sleeper!
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.416
She's like,
"I'm not working right now.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:31.885
Nobody's ringing the doorbell.
I'm not coming over there."

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:33.453
-She didn't get
her hair and makeup.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.522
She didn't get her blowout.

00:02:35.522 --> 00:02:38.324
-She's a little
Maltese Yorkie mix,

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:39.959
and she's my white guard dog.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.994
She protects me.

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:46.565
-Do you feel that there
is a dangerous element

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:49.569
to how police are portrayed
on television and in films?

00:02:50.536 --> 00:02:54.640
-I think that, you know,
I wish it was more truthful.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.442
There are some movies that
are very true.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.512
Like, I feel like "Training Day"
was like right on the money.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.213
See, I've dated many
police officers

00:03:01.213 --> 00:03:03.716
thinking I would be protected
and be safe that way.

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:06.385
Turns out no.

00:03:06.385 --> 00:03:09.188
Turns out mnh-mnh,
mnh-mnh, no.

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:10.990
But lesson learned.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.292
You know, you live, you learn.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:17.897
And, you know,
Hollywood is Hollywood.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:19.265
They're telling stories.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:22.969
And there's not always truth
in those stories.

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.271
And I'm sure there's, like,
bits and tidbits of truth.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:26.539
And I feel like --

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.208
I remember when I wanted to
be a police officer.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:30.709
-Sure.
-And then I --

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:33.679
I remember being 7, 8 years old,
like "I wanna be a police!"

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:36.883
'Cause, you know, TV portrays
them as upright citizens

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:40.152
that, you know, they're here
to protect and keep the peace.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:43.957
And then as I get older,
as I'm outside more,

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:46.125
especially when I went
into foster care,

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.294
it's not that.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.595
It's not that.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:54.433
I see them antagonizing,
creating drama amongst people.

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:56.168
I was talking to my friend
the other day.

00:03:56.168 --> 00:03:58.204
She's like, "Well, why do y'all
keep killing each other?

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.905
If you guys didn't
kill each other --"

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:01.440
Girl, if you don't shu--

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.242
I was like,
"If you don't shut up..."

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:06.412
First of all, let's break down
why people kill each other.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:07.547
And don't sit up here and act

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:08.915
like white people
don't kill each other,

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.750
'cause there is a whole channel
called the ID channel

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.853
that I can sit and watch
all day,

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.754
white people killing each other.

00:04:15.754 --> 00:04:17.223
And never will there be
a rerun.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:21.394
They show different
new murders every day.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.429
But you watch the news
for 30 minutes

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:26.665
and think we over here
slaughtering each other.

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.736
And if that's the case, we would
all be gone, wouldn't we?

